An environment supplies the base URI and headers for an API instance. Keep
credentials and other caller-supplied configuration here. Extend
`AbstractEnvironment` and implement `uri(): UriInterface`.

```php
namespace ExampleSdk\Environments;

use GuzzleHttp\Psr7\Uri;
use Psr\Http\Message\UriInterface;
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\Environments\AbstractEnvironment;
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\Headers\BearerTokenAuthorizationHeader;

final class LiveEnvironment extends AbstractEnvironment
{
    public function __construct(public readonly string $apiKey, array $headers = [])
    {
        parent::__construct($headers);
    }

    public function uri(): UriInterface
    {
        return new Uri('https://api.example.com/v1');
    }

    public function headers(): array
    {
        return [...parent::headers(), new BearerTokenAuthorizationHeader($this->apiKey)];
    }
}
```

The parent constructor accepts headers. `addHeader()` mutates that header list;
do not make the whole environment readonly when callers use this method.
An endpoint can read its environment through `$this->di->api()->environment()`.
For query-based authentication, build the query with `http_build_query()` and
PSR URI `withQuery()`. Preserve any existing query parameters.

> Use `YourApi\Headers` namespace

For building passing / using headers you can use pre-build (or create your own) classes that will allow stacking
headers into reusable classes.

When ever you want to pass headers in environment or return it in endpoint you are advised to use header classes but you
can return a map of headers 'key' => 'header' or 'key' => 'headers'.

**Available classes (fell free to PR more):**

- `new JsonContentTypeHeaders()`
- `new AcceptsJsonHeaders()`
- `new JsonHeaders()` (sets the content type and accepts json headers)
- `BearerTokenAuthorizationHeader(string $token)`

Each header class can chain other headers (check JsonHeaders file).

<code-group>
  <code-block label="Simple header" active>

  ```php
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\Interfaces\HeadersInterface;

class AcceptsJsonHeaders implements HeadersInterface
{
    public function headers(): array
    {
        return [
            'Accept' => 'application/json',
        ];
    }
}
  ```

  </code-block>
  <code-block label="Combining headers">

  ```php
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\Interfaces\HeadersInterface;

class JsonHeaders implements HeadersInterface
{
    public function headers(): array
    {
        return [
            new JsonContentTypeHeaders(),
            new AcceptsJsonHeaders(),
            'X-test' => 'test',
        ];
    }
}
  ```

  </code-block>
</code-group>

Create an environment for the API's URL and credentials. See
[Environments](/architecture/environments) for a complete `LiveEnvironment` example.

Extend `AbstractApi` and add a typed accessor for each endpoint:

```php
namespace ExampleSdk;

use ExampleSdk\Endpoints\Units\UnitsEndpoint;
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\AbstractApi;

final class ExampleApi extends AbstractApi
{
    public function headers(): array
    {
        return [];
    }

    public function units(): UnitsEndpoint
    {
        return $this->makeEndpoint(UnitsEndpoint::class);
    }
}
```

The API implements `headers(): array` for API-wide defaults. Return an empty
array when the environment and endpoints provide all headers. Keep credentials in the environment. Supply environment-wide headers through
its `headers()` method and operation-specific headers through endpoint calls.
The API receives an environment and an `ApiFactoryContract` through its inherited
constructor. See [Create endpoint](/start-building/create-endpoint) and
[Use your API](/start-building/use-it).

Supply your API's environment and factory through the application's composition
code. In Laravel, inject `MakeApiFactory` into the class that builds API clients:

```php
namespace ExampleSdk;

use ExampleSdk\Environments\LiveEnvironment;
use WrkFlow\ApiSdkBuilder\Actions\MakeApiFactory;

final class ExampleApiFactory
{
    public function __construct(private readonly MakeApiFactory $makeApiFactory)
    {
    }

    public function create(string $apiKey): ExampleApi
    {
        return new ExampleApi(
            environment: new LiveEnvironment($apiKey),
            factory: $this->makeApiFactory->execute(),
        );
    }
}
```

Outside Laravel, supply a compatible `SDKContainerFactoryContract` to
`MakeApiFactory`, or construct `ApiFactory` with all its required PSR services.
The factory needs request, client, stream, container, and response implementations.

Call `$api->units()->get('42')` to receive `UnitResponse`. Catch SDK exceptions at
the application's external API boundary. See [Responses](/architecture/responses)
for status handling and [Testing](/architecture/testing) for network-free checks.
